-- title:
--   Transport of a semilinear chart automorphism between Drinfeld charts
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, $A$ a discrete valuation domain whose maximal ideal is generated by $\varpi$, with $q$ and a further element $t$ in that maximal ideal. Let $W_0$ and $W$ be adically complete discrete valuation domains with ring maps $\sigma_0 : A \to W_0$, $\sigma : A \to W$ such that $\sigma_0\varpi$, resp. $\sigma\varpi$, generates the maximal ideal, and let $f_0,u_0,v_0$ over $W_0$ and $f,u,v$ over $W$ be power series in two variables with $u,v$ units and $f$ congruent to $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ in each case. Put $S_0 = W_0[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_0t)v_0 - f_0u_0)$ and $S = W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma t)v - fu)$, both assumed local, $S$ in addition Hausdorff for its maximal-ideal filtration, and let $J_0$, $J$ be the ideals generated by the images of $X_0,X_1$. Given a ring isomorphism $\beta : S_0 \simeq S$ matching the constants $C(\sigma_0a)$ with $C(\sigma a)$, a commutative ring $D$ with maps $\psi_0 : D \to S_0$, $\psi : D \to S$ satisfying $\beta\circ\psi_0 = \psi$ and with $\psi_0(D)$ dense in $S_0$ for the maximal-adic filtration, and assuming that every element of $W_0$ (resp. $W$) agrees with some $\sigma_0a$ (resp. $\sigma a$) up to a constant lying in the maximal ideal of $S_0$ (resp. $S$): suppose that for each $g \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$ there are an integral lift $G$ of $g$, an endomorphism $\tau_g$ of $D$, automorphisms $\theta_{0,g}$ of $S_0$ and $\theta_g$ of $S$ intertwining $\psi_0$, $\psi$ with $\tau_g$ and fixing all constants, whose linear parts $M_{0,g}$, $M_g$ (i.e. $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i$ modulo the square of $J_0$, resp. $J$) are congruent to $G$ modulo the maximal ideals of $W_0$, $W$. Suppose further that an endomorphism $\tau$ of $D$, an automorphism $\sigma_A$ of $A$, an element $x \in A$ and a matrix $N$ of natural numbers are realised on the first chart by data $\theta_0$, $\sigma_{W_0}$, $c_0 \in W_0$, $M_0$ with $\theta_0\circ\psi_0 = \psi_0\circ\tau$, $\sigma_{W_0}\circ\sigma_0 = \sigma_0\circ\sigma_A$, $\sigma_{W_0} \equiv \mathrm{id}$ modulo the maximal ideal, $\theta_0(C(w)) = C(\sigma_{W_0}w)$, linear part $M_0$ modulo $J_0^2$, $c_0 \equiv \sigma_0x$ and $M_{0,ij} \equiv c_0N_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W_0$. Then the same data exist on the second chart: there are $\theta : S \simeq S$, $\sigma_W : W \simeq W$, $c \in W$ and a matrix $M$ over $W$ with $\theta\circ\psi = \psi\circ\tau$, $\sigma_W\circ\sigma = \sigma\circ\sigma_A$, $\sigma_W \equiv \mathrm{id}$ modulo the maximal ideal, $\theta(C(w)) = C(\sigma_Ww)$, $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i$ modulo $J^2$, $c \equiv \sigma x$ and $M_{ij} \equiv cN_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$.
--
--   This is the purely algebraic form of the chart-transport step in the computation of the tame inertia action on a Drinfeld-level modular curve (Katz–Mazur 13.7–13.8): a semilinear automorphism with prescribed scalar linear part, known on one Drinfeld chart of a complete local ring, is carried over to a second chart sharing the same dense subring $D$ and the same $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$-action by constant-linear automorphisms. It is used in the full-level inertia computation for the Drinfeld chart, [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.inertia_drinfeldChart_semilinear_linearPart_transport_of_levelAut_linearPart_of_pow_eq_mul_of_isPrimitiveRoot_mul_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.inertia_drinfeldChart_semilinear_linearPart_transport_of_levelAut_linearPart_of_pow_eq_mul_of_isPrimitiveRoot_mul_of_dvd); the hypothesis $q \neq 2$ of the earlier version of the transport statement is absent here.
